Thanks Brian. Once I get past this test, my idea was to find an implementation of Akima that has a proper license, and provide a wrapper api to it. Then I'd have to evaluate how it works, test if it does what is advertised, and make any needed improvements. My skills tend to lay in the optimization of algorithms by proper design, and also following good software engineering practices. It looks like the Akima interpolation has been beat to death in terms of the amount of resources available in terms of implementations and scholarly work. On top of that, it sounds very interesting. I am familiar with econometrics and statistics so the interpolation is interesting to me in how it is used to fit data.

